 # Overview
 
-Agent-Pipeline is for controlled, agent-assisted software delivery where a team
-needs a reviewable record beyond a chat history. It turns an intent into a
-bounded change, deterministic evidence, independent review, and a preserved
-decision. Receipts bound to the candidate commit and validated packages can
-support an audit trail; they do not certify compliance or replace an auditor.
+Agent-Pipeline is for software teams that let AI agents write code and still
+have to show afterwards what was decided, what was checked and by whom. It turns
+an idea into a bounded change, deterministic checks, an independent review and a
+preserved decision. Its receipts are bound to the exact commit that was checked
+and can support an audit trail; they do not certify compliance and do not
+replace an auditor.
+
+## What you get
+
+- **A written design before code.** The agent drafts a requirements document
+  and a spec with you; an independent read-only reviewer reads them; you approve
+  once.
+- **Work cut into small, checkable pieces.** Each piece is built by a fresh agent
+  with a defined write scope, then checked by tests and security scans.
+- **An independent review of the result**, by an agent that never saw the
+  implementing agent's reasoning.
+- **A record you can hand over.** Receipts, decisions and review reports stay in
+  the repository and can be exported as an offline bundle.
+- **Humans decide at a few fixed points**: the design, the final acceptance and
+  every push. Everything in between is agent work.
+
+It works with Claude Code, Codex and Antigravity. The method is the same on all
+three; what each host can enforce natively differs and is stated per runner in
+[runner support](runner-support.md).
 
 ## The next release line: 0.7.0
 
-**Names.** *Alfred* is the working name of the `0.7.0` scope. *Phoenix* and
-*Nova* are the two product strands it integrates. *Sprint Alfred* is the
-maintainer sprint that builds the scope.
-
-`0.7.0` names the next distributed Alfred architecture scope. Until that
-version is released, an application repository continues to use its approved
-GitHub-distributed plugin version; a maintainer release candidate is not an
-installation recommendation, production-availability claim, or proof that
-release gates passed. The scope adds a staged adoption route for existing
-repositories, a machine-readable architecture map, baseline and fitness
-checks, and typed architecture impact at feature close. Where the installed
-plugin contains the architecture-adoption CLI (read back the installed plugin
-version and root in a fresh `pipeline-start` session first), begin with the
-read-only [adoption status and proposal](usage.md#assess-architecture-adoption-in-an-existing-project)
-in the repository you intend to govern.
-
-| Product strand | What it contributes to this release scope | Status |
-| --- | --- | --- |
-| Phoenix | Durable delivery governance: explicit human decisions, candidate-bound evidence, deterministic gates before review, safe external-action boundaries, and recoverable public records. | Integrated foundation |
-| Nova | Runner-aware execution and guided adoption: public [Driver](README.md#terms-used-across-these-documents) actions, resumable onboarding context, a real verify setup, and scoped parallel delivery. | Implemented in the repository; final `0.7.0` acceptance pending. |
-
-**Status.** The `0.7.0` release candidate is not yet qualified: it still needs
-criterion-level evidence, full Verify, security checks, independent review, and
-any applicable human acceptance, and host-dependent behavior needs readback from
-the runner that actually executes it. This overview claims neither a released
-nor a fully accepted `0.7.0`.
-
-The documented Greenfield route (empty directory to first implementation step)
-is covered for Claude, Codex, and Antigravity; see [Usage](usage.md), in
-particular [from an idea to the implementation phase](usage.md#from-an-idea-to-the-implementation-phase),
-and [what's new in 0.7.0](whats-new-0.7.0.md) for what the release candidate
-adds and what stays open. Runner
-integrations are described by their evidence: a common methodology does not
-mean that every host has identical native hooks, sandbox isolation, model
-identity, or platform coverage. Missing, stale, or malformed evidence, and
-evidence that does not match the candidate commit, stays a typed non-success.
-
-For the product entry point, use the top-level [README](../README.md); for
-everything else, the [documentation map](README.md). The
-[Operating Model](operating-model.md) is the normative process contract.
+`0.7.0` adds a guided path from an empty directory to a signed design and into
+implementation, a machine-readable architecture map with boundary checks, a
+quality readback at the close, and a staged route for adopting the architecture
+controls in an existing repository. What it contains is listed in
+[what's new in 0.7.0](whats-new-0.7.0.md).
+
+**Status.** `0.7.0` is not released. Until it is, an application repository
+keeps using the plugin version it already has approved; a pre-release build is
+not an installation recommendation and not a claim that the release checks
+passed. Full verification, the security checks, the independent review and the
+final human acceptance of `0.7.0` are still outstanding. The new path has been
+run end to end on all three runners, but those runs are not a release proof.
+
+## Where to start
+
+1. Read the top-level [README](../README.md), then [Setup](../SETUP.md).
+2. Follow [Usage](usage.md); for a new project start with
+   [from an idea to the implementation phase](usage.md#from-an-idea-to-the-implementation-phase),
+   for an existing one with the
+   [adoption assessment](usage.md#assess-architecture-adoption-in-an-existing-project).
+3. The limits you may meet are in [known issues](known-issues.md); everything
+   else is in the [documentation map](README.md). The
+   [Operating Model](operating-model.md) is the normative process contract.
+
+Missing, stale or mismatching evidence is always reported as a typed
+non-success, never counted as a pass.
